{
  "requirement": "The `habeas` CLI MUST use `clap` to define and parse its commands and options.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Verdict:** the sources satisfy `requirements.cli.clap`.\n\n**Implementation**\n- `Cargo.toml` depends on `clap` 4.6.7 with the `derive` feature.\n- `src/main.rs` imports `clap::{ArgGroup, Parser, Subcommand}`.\n- `struct Cli` derives `Parser` with `#[command(name = \"habeas\")]` and a `#[command(subcommand)] command: Command` field. It carries the `{impl}` marker for this requirement.\n- `enum Command`, `enum MdbookCommand` and `enum HookCommand` derive `Subcommand`. They define every command and option: `mdbook`, `mdbook supports`, `setup --path`, `status [--all] [glob]`, `info`, `author` (with `--all`, `--config`, `--stdout` and the `author-evidence` alias), the hidden `hook session-start`, and `add-input`. `author` and `add-input` use `ArgGroup`s for required and mutually exclusive targets.\n- `main()` parses with `Cli::parse().command`, so clap does all the parsing. The only `env::args` hit in `src/` is a text match inside a string; I found no manual argument parsing. The `.expect(\"clap requires …\")` calls rely on clap's group constraints.\n\n**Tests**\n- No test is tagged with `requirements.cli.clap`. This is a structural requirement, and the CLI tests exercise clap's behavior directly:\n  - `tests/cli.rs` runs the real binary with an unknown command, `setup --wrong` and `setup --install-skills`. It asserts a failure exit and clap's `Usage: habeas` message on stderr.\n  - `tests/commands.rs` (around line 250) asserts the same clap usage error for invalid argument combinations.\n  - `tests/cli.rs` also runs `mdbook supports html` and `mdbook supports epub`, which goes through the clap-parsed subcommands.\n- That coverage is adequate for a requirement this simple.",
